From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Gino D." <25200363+zinosaure@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 22:01:23 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] gameflex 0061: the synchronization follows the guest
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

On the console, the draws, dispatches and DMA transfers of a queue overlap
until the game asks the command processor to wait: ACQUIRE_MEM, a
CS/VS/PS_PARTIAL_FLUSH, a writeback of the color or depth caches, a DMA with
CP_SYNC, a WAIT_REG_MEM on a label the GPU has yet to write. Kyty ignored
those packets and surrounded every command that writes memory with two full
barriers (ALL_COMMANDS, MEMORY_READ|WRITE) instead: on RADV each one drains
compute and pixels, writes back and invalidates L2 and flushes the CB/DB
caches. About 3 000 barriers an image in Astro Bot's Celestial Garden, the
GPU idle a third of the time inside its own command buffers.

GuestSync, owned by the RenderContext, keeps the state of the stream: the
commands say what they wrote (draws whose shaders write, dispatches with
storage writes, transfers), the command processor says when the game waits
and for which stages, and the next command posts one barrier for all of it,
with the stages the game named as the source. Gone: the two barriers around
each dispatch and each transfer (transfers over the same bytes stay ordered,
as in the DMA engine's queue), the indirect-arguments barrier, the
repeated-write rule of the images, the run mechanism of gameflex 0060. The
labels of RELEASE_MEM are written with VK_AMD_buffer_marker when the device
has it: the console's end-of-pipe write, without a barrier. The end of a
command buffer releases what the run still owes.

Measured on Astro Bot in the Celestial Garden (RADV, Radeon 780M): the idle
time inside command buffers down 59 %, dispatches that found the GPU drained
99.6 → 44 %, labels 94 → 9.5 %, 5.27 → 5.53 images/s; Wukong unchanged (same
images, same GPU load). Clair Obscur lost the device until DMA_DATA's CP_SYNC
was honored.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
